## Write f(x) as a power
f(x) &= (5x^2 + 7)^{-1}
## Differentiate using the chain rule
Bring down the power, reduce it by 1, then multiply by the derivative of $5x^2 + 7$, which is $10x$.
f'(x) &= -1(5x^2 + 7)^{-2}(10x)
&= -10x(5x^2 + 7)^{-2}
## Write with a positive power
&= \frac{-10x}{(5x^2 + 7)^2}
ANSWER: f'(x) = \frac{-10x}{(5x^2 + 7)^2}

## Name the two factors and differentiate each
$g(x)$ is a product, so use the product rule with $u$ and $v$.
u &= \tan\frac{x}{2}
\frac{du}{dx} &= \frac12\sec^2\frac{x}{2}
v &= \ln x
\frac{dv}{dx} &= \frac1x
## Apply the product rule
$g'(x) = u\,\frac{dv}{dx} + v\,\frac{du}{dx}$
g'(x) &= \tan\frac{x}{2}\cdot\frac1x + \ln x\cdot\frac12\sec^2\frac{x}{2}
## Substitute x = π/2
g'\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right) &= \tan\frac{\pi}{4}\cdot\frac{1}{\pi/2} + \ln\frac{\pi}{2}\cdot\frac12\sec^2\frac{\pi}{4}
## Evaluate the trig values
$\tan\frac{\pi}{4} = 1$ and $\sec^2\frac{\pi}{4} = \frac{1}{(1/\sqrt{2})^2} = 2$.
&= 1\cdot\frac{2}{\pi} + \ln\frac{\pi}{2}\cdot\frac12\cdot 2
&= \frac{2}{\pi} + \ln\frac{\pi}{2}
ANSWER: g'\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right) = \frac{2}{\pi} + \ln\frac{\pi}{2}

## Maximum speed happens when v'(t) = 0
v'(t) &= 0
2t^2 - 12t + 13 &= 0
## Use the quadratic formula with a = 2, b = -12, c = 13
t &= \frac{-(-12) \pm \sqrt{(-12)^2 - 4(2)(13)}}{2(2)}
&= \frac{12 \pm \sqrt{144 - 104}}{4}
&= \frac{12 \pm \sqrt{40}}{4}
## Evaluate the two solutions
t &= \frac{12 + \sqrt{40}}{4}
&= 4.58
t &= \frac{12 - \sqrt{40}}{4}
&= 1.42
## Reject the value outside the first 4 minutes
$t = 4.58$ is outside $0 \le t \le 4$, so it is rejected. The only candidate is $t = 1.42$.
## Check it is a maximum using v''(t)
v''(t) &= 4t - 12
v''(1.42) &= 4(1.42) - 12
&= -6.32
$v''(1.42) < 0$, so the speed is a maximum at $t = 1.42$.
ANSWER: t = 1.42 \text{ minutes}

## Fastest legal journey is at 100 km/hour
The minimum time is when she drives at the maximum speed for the whole 10 km.
\text{time} &= \frac{\text{distance}}{\text{speed}}
&= \frac{10}{100}
&= \frac{1}{10} \text{ hour}
## Convert hours to minutes
\frac{1}{10} \times 60 &= 6
ANSWER: 6 \text{ minutes}

## Distance covered in the first 2 minutes
d &= 120 \times \frac{2}{60}
&= 4 \text{ km}
10 - 4 &= 6
So 6 km remain to get to $B$.
## Total time for an average of 100 km/hour
\text{total time} &= \frac{10}{100}
&= \frac{1}{10} \text{ hour}
&= 6 \text{ minutes}
6 - 2 &= 4
So the last 6 km take 4 minutes.
## Average speed over the last 6 km
\text{speed} &= \frac{6}{4/60}
&= 90
## Find the speed v at B
Constant deceleration means the average speed is the mean of the starting speed 120 and the final speed $v$.
\frac{120 + v}{2} &= 90
120 + v &= 180
v &= 60
## Deceleration over the 4 minutes
a &= \frac{120 - 60}{4}
&= 15
ANSWER: 15 \text{ km/hour per minute}

## Differentiate y = 12/x
y &= \frac{12}{x}
&= 12x^{-1}
\frac{dy}{dx} &= -12x^{-2}
&= -\frac{12}{x^2}
## Find the slope of the tangent at x = p
m &= -\frac{12}{p^2}
## Substitute the slope and the point into y - y1 = m(x - x1)
y - \frac{12}{p} &= -\frac{12}{p^2}(x - p)
## Multiply out the bracket and solve for y
y - \frac{12}{p} &= -\frac{12}{p^2}x + \frac{12p}{p^2}
y - \frac{12}{p} &= -\frac{12}{p^2}x + \frac{12}{p}
y &= -\frac{12}{p^2}x + \frac{12}{p} + \frac{12}{p}
y &= -\frac{12}{p^2}x + \frac{24}{p}
ANSWER: y = -\frac{12}{p^2}x + \frac{24}{p}

## Find the y-intercept (let x = 0)
y &= -\frac{12}{p^2}(0) + \frac{24}{p}
&= \frac{24}{p}
The height of the triangle is $\frac{24}{p}$.
## Find the x-intercept (let y = 0)
0 &= -\frac{12}{p^2}x + \frac{24}{p}
\frac{12}{p^2}x &= \frac{24}{p}
x &= \frac{24}{p} \times \frac{p^2}{12}
x &= 2p
The base of the triangle is $2p$.
## Use area = ½ × base × height
\text{area} &= \frac{1}{2}(2p)\left(\frac{24}{p}\right)
&= \frac{48p}{2p}
&= 24
ANSWER: k = 24

## Find the height of each rectangle
h &= 8 \div 3
&= \frac{8}{3}
## Count the small triangles sticking out of the big triangle
The rectangles cover the big triangle plus a small triangle on each side of each rectangle.
$T_3$ has 3 rectangles, so there are 6 small triangles. Their bases add up to the width 2 of the big triangle, so each base is $\frac{2}{6}$.
\text{base} &= \frac{2}{6}
&= \frac{1}{3}
## Area of the 6 small triangles
\text{Area} &= 6 \times \frac{1}{2} \times \frac{1}{3} \times \frac{8}{3}
&= \frac{8}{3}
## Area of the big triangle
\text{Area} &= \frac{1}{2} \times 2 \times 8
&= 8
## Add the two areas
\text{Total} &= \frac{8}{3} + 8
&= \frac{8}{3} + \frac{24}{3}
&= \frac{32}{3}
This is the required total area, in square units.
ANSWER: \text{Total area} = \frac{32}{3}

## Find the height of each rectangle
h &= \frac{8}{n}
## Count the small triangles and find their bases
$T_n$ has $n$ rectangles, so there are $2n$ small triangles. Their bases add up to 2.
\text{base} &= \frac{2}{2n}
&= \frac{1}{n}
## Area of the $2n$ small triangles
\text{Area} &= 2n \times \frac{1}{2} \times \frac{1}{n} \times \frac{8}{n}
&= \frac{8}{n}
## Add the area of the big triangle
The big triangle has area $\frac{1}{2} \times 2 \times 8 = 8$.
\text{Total} &= \frac{8}{n} + 8
&= \frac{8}{n} + \frac{8n}{n}
&= \frac{8(n+1)}{n}
ANSWER: \frac{8(n+1)}{n} = 8 + \frac{8}{n}

## Write the chain rule
We want $\frac{dS}{dt}$ and know $\frac{dx}{dt}$, so link them through $x$.
\frac{dS}{dt} &= \frac{dS}{dx} \times \frac{dx}{dt}
The rate at which $x$ increases is given.
\frac{dx}{dt} &= 3
## Differentiate $S$ with respect to $x$
S &= \frac{c^2}{h^2} x^2
\frac{dS}{dx} &= \frac{c^2}{h^2} (2x)
&= \frac{2c^2 x}{h^2}
## Multiply by $\frac{dx}{dt}$
\frac{dS}{dt} &= \frac{2c^2 x}{h^2} \times 3
&= \frac{6c^2 x}{h^2}
## Substitute $x = \frac{h}{2}$
\frac{dS}{dt} &= \frac{6c^2 \left(\frac{h}{2}\right)}{h^2}
&= \frac{3c^2 h}{h^2}
&= \frac{3c^2}{h}
ANSWER: \frac{dS}{dt} = \frac{3c^2}{h} \text{ square units per second}

## Find the probabilities of each amount
There are 12 equally likely sectors. Write $P(6)$ for landing on a €6 sector and $P(9)$ for a €9 sector.
P(6) &= \frac{5}{12}
P(9) &= \frac{3}{12}
## Multiply the three independent spins
Each spin is independent and the probabilities do not change.
P(6, 9, 6) &= \frac{5}{12} \times \frac{3}{12} \times \frac{5}{12}
&= \frac{75}{1728}
&= 0.04340\ldots
&\approx 0.0434
ANSWER: 0.0434

## Find the probabilities of success and failure
A success is getting €9.
P(\text{success}) &= \frac{3}{12}
&= \frac{1}{4}
P(\text{failure}) &= 1 - \frac{1}{4}
&= \frac{3}{4}
## Describe the event
The 3rd €9 comes on spin 8. So there are exactly 2 successes in the first 7 spins, then a success on spin 8.
P &= \binom{7}{2} \left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^2 \left(\frac{3}{4}\right)^5 \times \frac{1}{4}
## Evaluate each part
\binom{7}{2} &= 21
\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^2 &= \frac{1}{16}
\left(\frac{3}{4}\right)^5 &= \frac{243}{1024}
## Multiply
P &= 21 \times \frac{1}{16} \times \frac{243}{1024} \times \frac{1}{4}
&= \frac{5103}{65536}
&= 0.07786\ldots
&\approx 0.0779
ANSWER: 0.0779

## Find which totals are €16 or more
The possible amounts are €0, €6 and €9. The biggest two-spin total other than 9 and 9 is 9 + 6 = 15, which is under €16.
So the total is €16 or more only when both spins give €9 (a total of €18).
## Probability of two €9 spins
P(9, 9) &= \frac{1}{4} \times \frac{1}{4}
&= \frac{1}{16}
## Use the complement
P(\text{less than } €16) &= 1 - \frac{1}{16}
&= \frac{15}{16}
&= 0.9375
ANSWER: \frac{15}{16} = 0.9375

## Split 75° into 30° + 45°
\sin 75^\circ &= \sin(30^\circ + 45^\circ)
## Apply the formula from part (a)
&= \sin 30^\circ \cos 45^\circ + \cos 30^\circ \sin 45^\circ
## Substitute the exact values
$\sin 30^\circ = \frac{1}{2}$, $\cos 30^\circ = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$, $\sin 45^\circ = \cos 45^\circ = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}$.
&= \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} + \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
&= \frac{1}{2\sqrt{2}} + \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2\sqrt{2}}
&= \frac{1 + \sqrt{3}}{2\sqrt{2}}
## Rationalise the denominator
Multiply top and bottom by $\sqrt{2}$.
&= \frac{(1 + \sqrt{3})\sqrt{2}}{2\sqrt{2} \times \sqrt{2}}
&= \frac{(1 + \sqrt{3})\sqrt{2}}{4}
&= \frac{\sqrt{2} + \sqrt{6}}{4}
ANSWER: \sin 75^\circ = \frac{\sqrt{2} + \sqrt{6}}{4}

## Compare with the standard circle equation
A circle with centre $(a, b)$ and radius $r$ has equation $(x - a)^2 + (y - b)^2 = r^2$.
(x - h)^2 + (y + 3)^2 &= 12
(x - h)^2 + (y - (-3))^2 &= 12
## Read off the centre
Here $a = h$ and $b = -3$.
\text{Centre} &= (h, -3)
## Find the radius
r^2 &= 12
r &= \sqrt{12}
&= \sqrt{4 \times 3}
&= 2\sqrt{3}
ANSWER: \text{Centre} = (h, -3), \quad \text{Radius} = 2\sqrt{3}
